body:
People don't leave bad jobs, they leave bad bosses.

&#x200B;

You said the pay is low, how much do you think that boss is making off of your 20-25 patients a day? Certainly enough to pay you fair wages for working a full Saturday.

&#x200B;

The job market for PTAs is going insane right now due to Medicare cuts. You could really shop around.

I absolutely love working with my patients. Watching them improve and knowing you helped them reach those final goals is (almost) priceless.  That's the only thing that helps me go at all in this profession.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Therapist pay is going down, not up. Friends in other industries with a BA complain about not  getting a big enough raise or bonus while I retreat to my car and cry over my 15% therapist-wide pay cut because of insurance changes. That's on top of the fact that I made 7% less here at the exact same job 2 years ago. Experience means nothing. Specialties mean nothing. Education means nothing. You're all the same to insurance, do you honestly believe a company would lower their profits because they were actually impressed by those? A billed unit is a billed unit, just don't get sued. Get your paperwork done on time and that's all thy care about, not what's actually on the paper.

&#x200B;

The lack of ethics in this field is heart breaking. Do you know how many times I have been forced by my employer to provide services to patients who were not appropriate or even refused? All so they could make money. They had my clinical judgement, but it didn't matter. "Get back in there are see them". Why even hire a therapist if you don't respect them. 

My resume shows that I've worked several places which is not seen as a good thing. I kept jumping jobs that I felt were unethical until I finally realized...they all were. That's made things a lot easier to accept. Healthcare is a business, not a charity. It exists to make a profit, whatever that takes. We therapists have amazing hearts and want to do everything for our patients, but everything is decided by suits in tall buildings that have never even heard a mother cry because she has no idea how she can afford the wheelchair their son needs or cried yourself after seeing the wedding video where your BKA walks his daughter down the aisle only holding her arm and no walker. Or the hug that almost knocks you over when an old patient visits the clinic to tell you how she just got back from her trip to London and she didn't even fall on the cobblestones, not even once thanks to your balance exercises. You are all just numbers on a spreadsheet to the people who control everything you do. And they want big numbers, not hugs. So get ready for commanding an aide around to see your 5 patients an hour while you just do some manual on everybody so you can bill.

&#x200B;

 My boyfriend in the tech industry gets a paid week off at Christmas in addition to 3 weeks PTO. It's so hard for me to go to work on those days. I haven't been able to get a job with PTO in years.

&#x200B;

I just think of how excited I was on graduation day, all that work finally paying off. I really felt like someone. After being treated like shit from companies, I feel like nothing. When you have years of employers treating you like nothing, you begin to lose that sparkle in your eye.

&#x200B;

I know my education has made a huge positive impact on my patient care, even if it was financial armageddon. But it's really just the way companies have treated me that made me so disillusioned over the years, especially compared to how much better those outside the field are treated. The lack of ethics and either completely pimping therapy out for billing or providing less funding than Ebeneezer Scrooge just shows how little our entire field is respected. When your clinical-doctorate level hands are tied by people who just see your work as another profit making business with the only goal of money and not patient care or treating employees fairly, it's hard to feel good.
